BEGIN
 FOR entry IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements($examples$[{"id":"MG-G-EXAM-PCTCHANGE-1","qualification":"GCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Foundation","topic":"Number · Successive percentage changes","prompt":"A laptop has original price \\(\\pounds 100\\). Its price increases by \\(10\\%\\), then the new price is reduced by \\(10\\%\\). Find the final price in pounds and explain why the changes must be applied successively.","marks":5,"difficulty":3,"minutes":8,"scheme":"M1: Use the increase multiplier \\(\\frac{11}{10}\\).\nB1: Find intermediate price \\(\\pounds 110\\).\nB1: Use the reduction multiplier \\(\\frac{9}{10}\\).\nB1: Obtain final price \\(\\pounds 99\\).\nA1: Explain that each percentage is of the new price.\nAnswer: \\(\\pounds 99\\)"},{"id":"MG-G-EXAM-RATIO-1","qualification":"GCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Foundation","topic":"Ratio · Sharing and interpretation","prompt":"A community group shares \\(\\pounds 20\\) between Projects A and B in the ratio \\(2:3\\). Calculate both shares. A volunteer claims that each project receives the same amount; explain briefly why this is not correct.","marks":5,"difficulty":3,"minutes":7,"scheme":"M1: Add the ratio parts: \\(2+3=5\\).\nB1: Determine one part \\(\\pounds 4\\).\nB1: Calculate A = \\(\\pounds 8\\).\nB1: Calculate B = \\(\\pounds 12\\).\nA1: Explain the parts differ, so the allocations differ.\nAnswer: \\(A=\\pounds 8,\\quad B=\\pounds 12\\)"},{"id":"MG-G-EXAM-FENCE-1","qualification":"GCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Foundation","topic":"Geometry · Perimeter and money","prompt":"A rectangular garden is \\(5\\text{ m}\\) long and \\(3\\text{ m}\\) wide. Fencing costs \\(\\pounds 4\\) per metre. Calculate the perimeter and total fencing cost. Explain why calculating the area would not give the amount of fencing needed.","marks":5,"difficulty":3,"minutes":8,"scheme":"M1: Use the perimeter \\(P=2(5+3)\\).\nB1: Obtain \\(16\\text{ m}\\).\nB1: Multiply \\(16\\times 4\\).\nB1: Obtain \\(\\pounds 64\\).\nA1: Fencing follows the boundary length rather than covering the area.\nAnswer: \\(P=16\\text{ m},\\quad C=\\pounds 64\\)"},{"id":"MG-G-EXAM-TWODICE-1","qualification":"GCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Foundation","topic":"Probability · Two dice","prompt":"Two fair six-sided dice are rolled independently and their results are added. Calculate the probability that the sum is exactly \\(6\\). Explain why the sample space contains \\(36\\) ordered outcomes.","marks":4,"difficulty":3,"minutes":8,"scheme":"M1: List or count 5 ordered pairs summing to 6.\nB1: There are 6 × 6 equally likely ordered pairs.\nB1: Form \\(\\frac{5}{36}\\).\nA1: Explain that order matters: (a,b) and (b,a) are separate when a differs from b.\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{5}{36}\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-WEEKLY-BELLS-1","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"weekly","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Number · Lowest common multiple","prompt":"Two alarm lights flash together at noon. One flashes every 6 minutes and the other every 8 minutes. After how many minutes do they next flash together, and at what clock time will that occur? Show your method.","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"minutes":6,"scheme":"M1: Find multiples or prime factors of both periods.\nB1: Obtain \\(\\operatorname{LCM}(6,8)=24\\).\nB1: Use the LCM as the next simultaneous event.\nA1: State 24 minutes after noon, i.e. 12:24.\nAnswer: \\(24\\text{ minutes after noon}\\quad(\\text{at }12:24)\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-WEEKLY-PRICE-1","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"weekly","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Number · Percentage increase","prompt":"A bicycle costs \\(\\pounds 120\\) before a price increase of \\(10\\%\\). Find the amount of the increase and the new price. Explain which quantity the percentage is taken from.","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"minutes":6,"scheme":"M1: Find \\(10\\%\\) of \\(120\\).\nB1: Calculate increase \\(\\pounds 12\\).\nB1: Calculate new price \\(\\pounds 132\\).\nA1: The percentage is of the original price.\nAnswer: \\(\\pounds 132\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-WEEKLY-TRAPEZ-1","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"weekly","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Mensuration · Trapezia","prompt":"A trapezium has parallel sides of lengths \\(6\\text{ cm}\\) and \\(10\\text{ cm}\\), and perpendicular height \\(4\\text{ cm}\\). Calculate its area and include the correct units.","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"minutes":6,"scheme":"M1: Use \\(A=\\frac12(a+b)h\\).\nB1: Substitute \\(\\frac12(6+10)(4)\\).\nA1: Obtain \\(32\\text{ cm}^2\\).\nAnswer: \\(32\\text{ cm}^2\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-WEEKLY-MEAN-1","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"weekly","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Statistics · Missing data","prompt":"Five assessment scores are 4, 5, 6, 7 and \\(k\\). Their mean is \\(6.8\\). Calculate \\(k\\), clearly showing the total score required.","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"minutes":6,"scheme":"M1: Use total score \\(5\\times 6.8=34\\).\nB1: Find the sum \\(22\\) of known scores.\nA1: Subtract to obtain \\(k=12\\).\nAnswer: \\(k=12\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-EXAM-LADDER-1","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Geometry · Right triangles","prompt":"A straight ladder leans against a vertical wall. Its foot is \\(3\\text{ m}\\) from the wall, and its top touches the wall \\(4\\text{ m}\\) above the ground. Assuming the wall and ground form a right angle, calculate the ladder length. State the geometrical principle used.","marks":4,"difficulty":3,"minutes":8,"scheme":"M1: Recognise the ladder is the hypotenuse.\nB1: Use \\(L^2=3^2+4^2\\).\nB1: Find \\(L^2=25\\).\nA1: Obtain \\(5\\text{ m}\\).\nAnswer: \\(5\\text{ m}\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-EXAM-COSTEQ-1","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Algebra · Modelling","prompt":"A cinema charges \\(\\pounds 4\\) per ticket plus a single booking fee of \\(\\pounds 6\\). A total payment of \\(\\pounds 18\\) is made. Form and solve an equation to find how many tickets were purchased. Explain why the number of tickets must be an integer.","marks":5,"difficulty":3,"minutes":8,"scheme":"M1: Form \\(4n+6=18\\).\nB1: Subtract the booking fee first.\nB1: Divide by \\(4\\).\nB1: Obtain \\(n=3\\).\nA1: A ticket cannot be bought as a fractional item in this context.\nAnswer: \\(n=3\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-EXAM-REPLACE-1","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Probability · Independent draws","prompt":"A bag contains 2 red counters and 3 blue counters. Two counters are selected, and the first counter is replaced before the second selection. Calculate the probability of obtaining red twice and explain why the probabilities can be multiplied.","marks":4,"difficulty":3,"minutes":8,"scheme":"M1: First draw red probability \\(\\frac{2}{5}\\).\nB1: Replacement keeps the total and composition unchanged.\nB1: Multiply \\(\\frac{2}{5}\\times\\frac{2}{5}\\).\nA1: Obtain \\(\\frac{4}{25}\\).\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{4}{25}\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-EXAM-CYLSURF-1","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Mensuration · Cylinders","prompt":"A closed right circular cylinder has radius \\(2\\text{ cm}\\) and height \\(4\\text{ cm}\\). Find both its exact volume and its total surface area in terms of \\(\\pi\\). Show the formulae used and give units.","marks":4,"difficulty":3,"minutes":9,"scheme":"M1: Use \\(V=\\pi r^2h\\).\nB1: Calculate volume \\(16\\pi\\text{ cm}^3\\).\nB1: Use \\(S=2\\pi r^2+2\\pi rh\\).\nA1: Calculate surface area \\(24\\pi\\text{ cm}^2\\).\nAnswer: \\(V=16\\pi\\text{ cm}^3,\\quad S=24\\pi\\text{ cm}^2\\)"},{"id":"MG-G-EXAM-PCTCHANGE-2","qualification":"GCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Foundation","topic":"Number · Successive percentage changes","prompt":"A laptop has original price \\(\\pounds 200\\). Its price increases by \\(15\\%\\), then the new price is reduced by \\(20\\%\\). Find the final price in pounds and explain why the changes must be applied successively.","marks":5,"difficulty":3,"minutes":8,"scheme":"M1: Use the increase multiplier \\(\\frac{23}{20}\\).\nB1: Find intermediate price \\(\\pounds 230\\).\nB1: Use the reduction multiplier \\(\\frac{4}{5}\\).\nB1: Obtain final price \\(\\pounds 184\\).\nA1: Explain that each percentage is of the new price.\nAnswer: \\(\\pounds 184\\)"},{"id":"MG-G-EXAM-RATIO-2","qualification":"GCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Foundation","topic":"Ratio · Sharing and interpretation","prompt":"A community group shares \\(\\pounds 35\\) between Projects A and B in the ratio \\(3:4\\). Calculate both shares. A volunteer claims that each project receives the same amount; explain briefly why this is not correct.","marks":5,"difficulty":3,"minutes":7,"scheme":"M1: Add the ratio parts: \\(3+4=7\\).\nB1: Determine one part \\(\\pounds 5\\).\nB1: Calculate A = \\(\\pounds 15\\).\nB1: Calculate B = \\(\\pounds 20\\).\nA1: Explain the parts differ, so the allocations differ.\nAnswer: \\(A=\\pounds 15,\\quad B=\\pounds 20\\)"},{"id":"MG-G-EXAM-FENCE-2","qualification":"GCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Foundation","topic":"Geometry · Perimeter and money","prompt":"A rectangular garden is \\(6\\text{ m}\\) long and \\(4\\text{ m}\\) wide. Fencing costs \\(\\pounds 5\\) per metre. Calculate the perimeter and total fencing cost. Explain why calculating the area would not give the amount of fencing needed.","marks":5,"difficulty":3,"minutes":8,"scheme":"M1: Use the perimeter \\(P=2(6+4)\\).\nB1: Obtain \\(20\\text{ m}\\).\nB1: Multiply \\(20\\times 5\\).\nB1: Obtain \\(\\pounds 100\\).\nA1: Fencing follows the boundary length rather than covering the area.\nAnswer: \\(P=20\\text{ m},\\quad C=\\pounds 100\\)"},{"id":"MG-G-EXAM-TWODICE-2","qualification":"GCSE","programme":"exam","exam_tier":"Foundation","topic":"Probability · Two dice","prompt":"Two fair six-sided dice are rolled independently and their results are added. Calculate the probability that the sum is exactly \\(7\\). Explain why the sample space contains \\(36\\) ordered outcomes.","marks":4,"difficulty":3,"minutes":8,"scheme":"M1: List or count 6 ordered pairs summing to 7.\nB1: There are 6 × 6 equally likely ordered pairs.\nB1: Form \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\).\nA1: Explain that order matters: (a,b) and (b,a) are separate when a differs from b.\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-WEEKLY-BELLS-2","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"weekly","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Number · Lowest common multiple","prompt":"Two alarm lights flash together at noon. One flashes every 8 minutes and the other every 12 minutes. After how many minutes do they next flash together, and at what clock time will that occur? Show your method.","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"minutes":6,"scheme":"M1: Find multiples or prime factors of both periods.\nB1: Obtain \\(\\operatorname{LCM}(8,12)=24\\).\nB1: Use the LCM as the next simultaneous event.\nA1: State 24 minutes after noon, i.e. 12:24.\nAnswer: \\(24\\text{ minutes after noon}\\quad(\\text{at }12:24)\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-WEEKLY-PRICE-2","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"weekly","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Number · Percentage increase","prompt":"A bicycle costs \\(\\pounds 160\\) before a price increase of \\(15\\%\\). Find the amount of the increase and the new price. Explain which quantity the percentage is taken from.","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"minutes":6,"scheme":"M1: Find \\(15\\%\\) of \\(160\\).\nB1: Calculate increase \\(\\pounds 24\\).\nB1: Calculate new price \\(\\pounds 184\\).\nA1: The percentage is of the original price.\nAnswer: \\(\\pounds 184\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-WEEKLY-TRAPEZ-2","qualification":"IGCSE","programme":"weekly","exam_tier":"Core","topic":"Mensuration · Trapezia","prompt":"A trapezium has parallel sides of lengths \\(7\\text{ cm}\\) and \\(11\\text{ cm}\\), and perpendicular height \\(5\\text{ cm}\\). Calculate its area and include the correct units.","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"minutes":6,"scheme":"M1: Use \\(A=\\frac12(a+b)h\\).\nB1: Substitute \\(\\frac12(7+11)(5)\\).\nA1: Obtain \\(45\\text{ cm}^2\\).\nAnswer: \\(45\\text{ cm}^2\\)"},{"id":"MG-I-